        "title": "Wetlands: Classification, Hydrology, Blue Carbon, Ecosystem Services",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Wetlands are defined by three co-occurring features: hydric soils, hydrophytic vegetation, and wetland hydrology. They are the most productive ecosystems per unit area on Earth, the largest single source of atmospheric methane, and the most effective per-hectare carbon sinks when preserved. They occupy a physical continuum from upland to deep water and are classified by multiple overlapping systems."
